# pots v1.4 — a posture stability sample from the standing phase.
#
# THIS CLASS HAS NO sh:Violation CONSTRAINT AT ALL. pots:PostureStabilityShape
# declares three: pots:isStable at sh:Warning, pots:stabilityScore and
# pots:atSeconds at sh:Info. Nothing it can report rises to Violation severity.
#
# That has two consequences worth stating, because both are easy to get wrong.
#
# FIRST, this class CANNOT have an .INVALID.ttl fixture. There is no constraint
# to violate, so any graph written for it -- however malformed -- produces no
# sh:Violation and the fixture fails with NO_VIOLATION. jayostis/conformance#10's
# acceptance criteria originally asked for a negative per covered class; that is
# unachievable here, and the issue was corrected before delivery rather than the
# criterion being quietly skipped.
#
# SECOND, a .VALID.ttl for this class asserts very little on its own. It says no
# violation was reported, which would remain true if pots:PostureStabilityShape
# did not exist. The claim that the shape FIRES is made by
# posturestability-isstable-repeated.WARN.ttl beside this file, which is the only
# polarity that can make it. This fixture's job is narrower and still worth
# having: it shows the well-formed record the warning fixture departs from, and
# it is what a producer should be writing.
#
# A stability score of 0.94 with isStable true is a clean standing phase -- the
# accelerometer saw very little sway. That matters clinically: an unstable posture
# during a NASA Lean test inflates heart rate through movement artefact rather
# than orthostatic response, which is why the check records this at all.
#
# Fully synthetic.

@prefix pots: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/pots/v1#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:uuid:p0t5-0008-4a00-8000-00000000ps10> a pots:PostureStability ;
    pots:isStable true ;
    pots:stabilityScore "0.94"^^xsd:double ;
    pots:atSeconds "300"^^xsd:integer .
